This project involves a watercraft - a so-called Diver-Purpose Vehicle (DPV). It is controlled by an ESP32, a VESC and other components.


You can find information about the project in my blog: https://bubtec.de/2024/10/29/diy-scooter-dpv-mit-aquazepp-und-dpvcontrol/



Tasks:
- Remember to update the API documentation (api-specification.yaml) every time you do something on the webserver and increment the api version!
- If you install serial logging, make sure you use the existing function for this!
- Always compile independently for major changes
- The code and comments are always in English!
- For every change, include in the chat a suggested git commit message in English.
- Always run and update tests when making code changes (python -m platformio test -e native or run_all_tests.ps1 to test all)
- Tests must be adapted when API or functionality changes
- Test failures must be fixed before proceeding with changes 
- Dont ask to test something. just test it! If you find an error, fix it without asking!
- If you've fixed a bug, check to see if a test already exists for it. If not, see if you can implement one.
- If, during the course of this project, you have learned anything relevant to it that should be incorporated into these rules, please let me know.




// Test automation rules
When any file in src/ is modified:
  - Run all tests in test/ directory
  - If tests fail, show error message and prevent commit

// API test coverage rules
When modifying any API endpoint in src/webserver.cpp:
  - Ensure corresponding test exists in test/test_api.cpp
  - Test must cover both success and error cases
  - Test must validate JSON response structure

// Mock implementation rules
When adding new hardware dependencies:
  - Create corresponding mock implementation in test/mock_*.h
  - Update mock_hardware.cpp with new mock functions
  - Ensure mocks are used in tests

// Test structure rules
When adding new tests:
  - Place in appropriate test/ subdirectory
  - Include setup/teardown functions
  - Use Unity test framework
  - Follow existing test naming conventions

// Hardware abstraction rules
When modifying hardware interactions:
  - Use mock implementations for testing
  - Keep hardware-specific code in separate files
  - Document hardware dependencies 